# Go conventions

The global Go rules in `~/.agents/rules/go.md` apply. This file adds what is
specific to this repository.

## Errors

- Wrap with `fmt.Errorf("...: %w", err)`. The message names the operation that
  failed, not the obvious (`"read issue %q: %w"`, not `"error: %w"`).
- Never discard an error. `_ =` requires a comment saying why it is safe.
- Domain errors are typed in `internal/errx` and carry their exit code. A
  command returns an error; it never calls `os.Exit` itself.
- Errors crossing a package boundary are translated, not leaked. A cobra command
  must never surface a raw `*url.Error` to the user.

## Resources

- `defer resp.Body.Close()` immediately after a successful request, every time.
- Drain the body before closing when the connection should be reused.
- Cancel every context you create. `defer cancel()` on the same line block.

## Types

- Explicit return types; no naked returns outside three-line functions.
- Accept interfaces, return structs. `CredentialStore` is an interface because
  tests substitute it; the Jira client is a struct.
- Zero values should be usable, or the constructor should be the only way to
  build the type. Do not ship a struct that panics when used unconstructed.

## Concurrency

- Do not add concurrent fan-out without an explicit bound and cancellation.
- Every goroutine has a defined exit path tied to the context.
- `go test -race` must pass. A data race is a blocking defect, not a flake.

## What not to do

- No package-level mutable state. Dependencies are passed explicitly.
- No `init()` beyond trivial registration.
- No `panic()` for expected conditions — return an `errx` error.
- No `interface{}`/`any` where a concrete type or a small interface fits.
